    <!-- Red Hat Display/Text/Mono, served from this origin (2026-08-05). This replaced two
         preconnects and a remote webfont stylesheet — the last external request the built site
         made. The theme's site.css names these families but ships no @font-face, so that remote
         stylesheet was their only source: with egress blocked (the Showroom cockpit serves from
         in-cluster) the page fell back to helvetica AND stalled on a DNS/TCP timeout on every
         load. Provenance, the variable-font weight trap and the refresh recipe are in
         css/vendor/red-hat-fonts/README.md. Deliberately no domain named here: it keeps
         "does the built site still reference the CDN?" a one-line grep over www/. -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{{uiRootPath}}}/css/vendor/red-hat-fonts/red-hat-fonts.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{{uiRootPath}}}/css/site.css">
    <script defer src="{{{uiRootPath}}}/js/click-to-run.js"></script>
    <script defer src="{{{uiRootPath}}}/js/lightbox.js"></script>
<style>
/* ── Click-to-run ▶ button (click-to-run.js adds it to every role=execute block) ──────────────
   Sits at the top-right of the code block, left of the theme's copy button, so attendees run a
   command in the terminal without copy-paste. */
.doc .listingblock.execute { position: relative; }
.run-in-terminal-btn {
  position: absolute; top: 0.35rem; right: 2.6rem; z-index: 3;
  font: 600 0.72rem/1 var(--body-font-family, sans-serif);
  color: #fff; background: #c9190b; border: 0; border-radius: 4px;
  padding: 0.28rem 0.55rem; cursor: pointer; opacity: 0.9;
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.25rem;
}
.run-in-terminal-btn:hover { opacity: 1; background: #a30000; }
.run-in-terminal-btn--flash { background: #3e8635; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { .run-in-terminal-btn { background: #e00; } }

/* ── Workshop overrides (root-caused live in-browser, 2026-07-10 project-owner review) ─────────────
   Two theme↔content collisions, both fixed here rather than in the theme bundle. */

/* 1) Mermaid diagrams rendered ~300px in an 828px column (unreadable). Cause: the theme's
   .imageblock is display:flex, so the .mermaid div is a shrink-to-fit flex ITEM whose child
   svg is width:100% — circular sizing collapses to min-content. Give the item the full column. */
.doc .imageblock .mermaid { width: 100%; flex: 1 1 100%; }
/* mermaid's useMaxWidth caps each SVG at its NATURAL size, so simple diagrams (few nodes) still
   render tiny — unreadable, especially in the narrow (~45%) cockpit panel (project-owner review
   2026-07-11, M01 concept). Override the inline max-width so every diagram scales UP to fill the
   column, capped at 900px on the wide GH-Pages site and centered. viewBox keeps it proportional. */
.doc .imageblock .mermaid svg {
  width: 100% !important; max-width: 900px !important; height: auto !important;
  display: block; margin: 0 auto;
}
/* Click-to-enlarge affordance (lightbox.js opens a full-window overlay — the overlay itself is
   inline-styled by the script since it mounts in the top document). CC-5: readable diagrams,
   worst-case the narrow cockpit panel. */
.doc .imageblock .mermaid, .doc .imageblock img { cursor: zoom-in; }

/* 2) Steps inside [tabs] panels rendered as overlapping black-bordered boxes. Cause: the
   theme ships tab-BAR styling with broad selectors (.tabs li / .tabs ul — it expects .tabs
   to be the bar element inside its own .tabset dialect), but the antora-tabs-extension
   classes the WHOLE openblock "tabs", so every content list in every PANEL got flex,
   height:2.5rem, border:1px solid black, bold, pointer. Neutralize inside panels only —
   the real bar (.tablist) keeps its styling. */
.openblock.tabs .tabpanel ul,
.openblock.tabs .tabpanel ol {
  display: block; flex-wrap: initial; list-style: revert; margin: revert; padding: revert;
}
.openblock.tabs .tabpanel li {
  display: list-item; align-items: initial; border: 0; cursor: auto; font-weight: inherit;
  height: auto; line-height: inherit; margin-right: 0; padding: 0; position: static;
}
.openblock.tabs .tabpanel li + li { margin-top: revert; }

/* ── 3) DEMO flavor: Say/Show/Do beat separators + the measured timing chip ────────────────────
   Every beat in every demo arc is authored as TWO block-attribute lines:

       [demo-block]
       [TIME 3m]
       Say:: …

   Asciidoctor's block STYLE is positional attribute 1, so the second line REPLACES the first:
   `demo-block` never reaches the HTML at all (verified 2026-08-05: 104 [demo-block] lines in
   source, 0 occurrences of that class anywhere in www/demo), and the surviving style is emitted
   verbatim into the wrapper div's class attribute — which therefore carries `dlist`, `TIME` and
   the bare value (`3m`) as three separate classes, for which neither the RHDP theme's site.css nor
   this file carried any rule. Net effect: 104 beats across all 26 demo arcs rendered as an
   undifferentiated wall of description lists, with the presenter's measured pacing invisible on
   every one. (demo-flavor review finding, 2026-08-05.)

   Deliberately NOT quoting the rendered class attribute literally anywhere in this comment: this
   file is inlined into every page of every flavor, so a literal copy plants one false hit per page
   in the very grep someone runs to ask "do demo beats leak into the workshop build?". Two drafts
   of this comment did contain it (once in prose, once inside a quoted grep pattern) and each
   produced 131 phantom hits per flavor, which read as a leak until run down. Grep for the opening
   DIV TAG, not the class attribute — that cannot match a stylesheet or a comment. Ground truth as
   of 2026-08-05: demo 104 beat elements, workshop 0, instructor 0.

   Fixed HERE rather than in the pages because the classes that DO land are perfectly usable, and
   styling them needs no edit to any of the 26 lab.adoc files. LOAD-BEARING CONSEQUENCE: `[TIME Nm]`
   is now the hook these rules key on — do not "tidy" it into a role, and do not reorder the two
   attribute lines (putting [demo-block] second would swap which one survives and silently kill
   every chip below). The now-inert `[demo-block]` line above each beat may be deleted as dead
   source; nothing here depends on it.

   DEMO-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION: every [TIME] line sits inside `ifdef::demo[]` (re-checked 106/106
   at source, 2026-08-12, after the agentic-ai arc grew two beats), so no `.dlist.TIME` element
   exists in the workshop or instructor renderings and these rules cannot affect them.
   Do not hand-maintain that count here — `tools/lint/demo-beat-chip-guard.py` enforces the
   invariant on every push, and a number written in a comment rots the moment a beat is added.

   `.\3N m` is the CSS escape for a class name starting with a digit (the trailing space
   terminates the hex escape).

   THE ENUMERATION BELOW IS THE SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR WHICH VALUES PAINT, and it is enforced
   rather than merely documented. An enumeration is not a style choice here: CSS cannot read a
   class name into `content:` — `attr()` yields a whole attribute value and CSS has no substring
   operator — so a generic rule that prints back whatever `[TIME Nm]` says is not expressible
   against this HTML, and listing the values is the only shape available.

   Every enumeration has an edge, so the edge is GATED rather than widened: a `[TIME Nm]` this file
   paints no rule for fails CI in `tools/lint/demo-beat-chip-guard.py` (finding `unpainted-value`),
   which is why the range below is not headroom-padded — an unpainted value cannot ship quietly, so
   there is nothing to buy by pre-painting durations no beat should have.

   THE PAIRING, and it is the fragile part. That guard needs the same set of values this file
   enumerates. The PAINTED-VALUES markers below exist so it can READ them from here and have no
   copy of its own — one list, one edit, and a rule deleted here then reddens CI on the pages that
   used it, which is the direction that otherwise goes unseen (a chip whose rule was removed still
   renders, just bare). If that guard still carries its own hard-coded list when you read this,
   these two are duplicates: editing the enumeration below means editing `PAINTED_VALUE` there in
   the SAME commit, and the drift you would otherwise cause is silent in both directions.

   So: do not move, reword or delete the two marker comments, and do not rewrite the `content:`
   strings into another shape — they are the parse. A parser that cannot find them must report that
   it could not inspect, never an empty set: empty would flag every chip in the tree and read as
   "the content broke" when the truth is "the guard could not read this file".

   Deliberately no range written in prose: a number in a comment rots, and the rules below already
   say what they cover. Values are minutes; a seconds value has no rule ON PURPOSE — that guard's
   canary uses `90s` as its witness that an unpainted value is caught, so painting it here would
   blind that detector and the self-test would fail.

   A value with no rule is not invisible — it still gets the separator and a bare ⏱ glyph, which
   is the failure mode this file prefers — but the beat's pacing is gone, and the gate above is
   what makes that loud instead of leaving it to be noticed on stage. */
.doc .dlist.TIME {
  position: relative;
  margin-top: 1.6rem;
  padding: 1.5rem 0.9rem 0.35rem;
  border-top: 2px solid #c9190b;
  border-left: 3px solid rgba(201, 25, 11, 0.25);
  background: rgba(201, 25, 11, 0.025);
}
.doc .dlist.TIME::before {
  content: "⏱";
  position: absolute; top: 0.3rem; left: 0.9rem;
  font: 600 0.72rem/1 var(--body-font-family, sans-serif);
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  color: #fff; background: #c9190b;
  border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.24rem 0.5rem;
}
/* PAINTED-VALUES-BEGIN — the painted set, in a shape tools/lint/demo-beat-chip-guard.py can parse:
   one rule per value, `content:` ending in that bare value. Add a line to paint a new duration.
   See THE PAIRING above before you do. */
.doc .dlist.TIME.\31 m::before  { content: "⏱ 1m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\32 m::before  { content: "⏱ 2m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\33 m::before  { content: "⏱ 3m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\34 m::before  { content: "⏱ 4m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\35 m::before  { content: "⏱ 5m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\36 m::before  { content: "⏱ 6m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\37 m::before  { content: "⏱ 7m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\38 m::before  { content: "⏱ 8m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\39 m::before  { content: "⏱ 9m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\31 0m::before { content: "⏱ 10m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\31 1m::before { content: "⏱ 11m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\31 2m::before { content: "⏱ 12m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\31 3m::before { content: "⏱ 13m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\31 4m::before { content: "⏱ 14m"; }
.doc .dlist.TIME.\31 5m::before { content: "⏱ 15m"; }
/* PAINTED-VALUES-END */
/* Say/Show/Do labels: the beat's three moves should scan at a glance from the stage. */
.doc .dlist.TIME > dl > dt { font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.01em; }
@media print {
  .doc .dlist.TIME { background: none; break-inside: avoid; }
}
</style>
